course: dictionary
lesson: 9835
title: One word, many meanings: swim
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The word swim can mean moving through water, a short time in the water, or feeling dizzy. Context tells you which meaning is meant. «Fish swim using their fins and tails» is about moving through water, because fish and fins are the clues.
explain: Look at the words around swim. «Pool rules are about slipping, pushing, drains and never swimming without a grown-up watching.» Here swimming is something you do in the water, and the rule tells you it is about safety.
explain: Words also change their form. Swim, swims, swam, swum and swimming all come from the same verb. «Yesterday I swam ten laps» uses swam because it happened in the past. Swum needs a helper word such as have: «I have swum ten laps.»
explain: When you are not sure, swap your meaning into the sentence. If «my head is swimming» means you feel dizzy, does the sentence still make sense? It does, so that is the meaning. Use this check for any new word.
fact: Swim can be a verb (to swim) or a noun (a swim, as in go for a swim).
fact: «Swim» as a verb means to move through water using your arms and legs, or your fins and tail.
fact: «My head is swimming» means you feel dizzy, not that you are in water.
fact: Fish swim using their fins and tails, and many fish can regrow damaged fins.
fact: In maths, laps or metres swum in a set time give a rate, so swimming 10 laps in 20 minutes tells you how fast you went.
exercise: «Fish swim using their fins and tails.» What does swim mean here? | *To move through water using their bodies | A short time in the pool | To sleep under the water | To eat food from the bottom
exercise: Mia says, «My head is swimming after the spinning ride.» What does she mean? | She is in the pool | She has lost her hat | She has learnt a new stroke | *She feels dizzy
exercise: Which word fits the gap? «Yesterday Sam ___ across the lake.» | swimming | *swam | swim | swims
test: Which word is a noun in this sentence? «Let us go for a swim after school.» | *swim | school | after | go
test: «Pool rules are about slipping, pushing, drains and never swimming without a grown-up watching.» Why do the rules mention swimming without a grown-up? | It is a funny rule about jokes | It is about fish in the pool | *It is about safety, so nobody swims alone | It is about the colour of the water
exam: Which sentence uses swum correctly? | *I have swum twenty lengths this week. | I have swam twenty lengths this week. | I have swim twenty lengths this week. | I swum twenty lengths yesterday.
